# ==============================================================================
# Weekly, Sunday - five attempts across the morning
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The OS stages a deployment nightly. Applying one nightly would mean the machine
# reboots most nights of the week, which is a lot of reboots on a box with no
# console to buy an update that was not urgent. Weekly still means no deployment
# sits unapplied for more than seven days, which was the actual problem: the host
# ran a superseded image for as long as nobody was at a keyboard.
#
# FIVE ATTEMPTS RATHER THAN ONE, because the likeliest refusal is transient and a
# single instant turned an hour-long inconvenience into a week-long one. Every
# check in bin/reboot-when-staged.sh is a reason not to reboot; the one that
# actually fires in normal operation is "the encoder is busy", and Tdarr jobs run
# for tens of minutes. A transcode finishing at 05:30 should not cost the
# deployment seven days. Refusals are still refusals - they simply get another
# look at 06:00. Four of the five are ordinarily no-ops that exit at the first
# gate, which costs nothing.
#
# 05:00-09:00 Sunday is clear of everything else that runs here:
#
#   00:00  podman-auto-update, three attempts to 02:00, restarting containers as
#          it goes - see host/systemd/podman-auto-update.timer.d/
#   00:00  rpm-ostreed-automatic, staging the deployment this may apply
#   03:00  home-server-backup, +5min jitter, TimeoutStartSec=45min
#   22:00  home-server-caddy-build, Saturdays
#
# So the backup has finished with an hour to spare even at its 45 minute timeout,
# and the reboot lands on the morning most likely to be noticed if it goes wrong.
# The script gates on that margin as well rather than trusting it: an After= in
# the service orders nothing against a backup that is already running.
#
# Persistent=false, which is the opposite of the backup timer and for the
# opposite reason. A missed backup is a hole and must be caught up; a missed
# reboot is simply next week, and catching one up means a machine that was off
# all weekend reboots itself the moment it comes back - exactly when someone is
# most likely to be doing something to it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A SECOND WINDOW, FOR A CRITICAL ADVISORY ONLY, AND IT STARTS AN HOUR LATER
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# deploy.image_age gives a staged CRITICAL advisory a THREE-DAY deadline and the
# only unattended path to apply one was weekly, so a critical landing on a Monday
# warned for three of the six days before this timer next fired and the remedy was
# always a person running bin/reboot-host.sh. ESCALATE_STAGED_D in the script
# relaxes the ENCODER gate; it does not add a window. Measured on 2026-09-11: 23
# days behind, one critical staged, /boot with room, nothing refusing, every
# signal green.
#
# THE TIMER CANNOT BE CONDITIONAL, so the calendar widens and the SCRIPT refuses.
# reboot-when-staged.sh exits 0 on any weekday morning whose staged deployment
# carries no critical advisory, before the expensive gates and without recording a
# refusal - it is the absence of work, the same category refuse() already exempts
# nothing_staged for.
#
# 06 AND NOT 05, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS NOT A ONE-LINE CHANGE. Four
# timers were placed at 05:20-05:40 SPECIFICALLY to sit outside this window, on
# the assumption it was Sunday-only, and three of them say so in their own
# headers: verify-segmentation Tue 05:20, verify-restore Wed 05:30,
# verify-restore-offsite first Mon 05:30 (~4.6 GB of egress), verify-media Thu
# 05:40 (a ~25-minute library sweep). Starting at 06:00 preserves the property all
# four were placed for, and costs one attempt - four rather than five. Adding four
# refusals to the script instead would be four new failure modes for a constraint
# one character already satisfies.
#
# Persistent=false IS ALREADY SET BELOW, so widening this calendar does NOT fire
# it on the next daemon-reload. That trap is real and this repository has paid for
# it - a Persistent=true timer's new schedule creates missed elapses in the past
# and elapses immediately - but it belongs to the other kind of timer. Said out
# loud because the next reader will reasonably worry.
# ==============================================================================

[Unit]
Description=Window for applying a staged OS deployment: weekly, or any morning for a critical advisory

[Timer]
# The jitter is well inside the hourly gap, so the attempts cannot collide
# or reorder.
OnCalendar=Sun *-*-* 05..09:00
OnCalendar=Mon..Sat *-*-* 06..09:00
Persistent=false
RandomizedDelaySec=10min
AccuracySec=1min

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
